\name{sdrf_restrict}
\alias{sdrf_restrict}
\title{Restrict an SDRF to the files one search read, and report what could not be placed}
\usage{
sdrf_restrict(path, searched_files, out = NULL, limit = NULL, offset = 0, timeout = 60)
}
\arguments{
\item{path}{Path to a \code{.sdrf.tsv} file.}

\item{searched_files}{The files the search read, as paths or bare names, a character vector.
  \strong{Name an element by the acquired file it came from} when you know it -
  \code{c("X-calib.mzML", "X.raw" = "sample_A.mzML")} - and that overrides the suffix rule for it;
  leave the others unnamed. Sent on stdin, one line each.}

\item{out}{Also write the \strong{whole} restricted document here as SDRF, whatever \code{limit} and
  \code{offset} are. Must end in \code{.tsv}; name it \code{.sdrf.tsv}, because mzLib adds that to a name that
  does not end in it. \code{written$path} names the file written.}

\item{limit}{Maximum rows to return. \code{NULL}, the default, returns all of them.}

\item{offset}{Rows to skip.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Restrict an SDRF to the files one search read, joining each searched file to the row of its acquisition, and report what the join could not place.

Answers \strong{which rows of this SDRF did my search read?} A search reads \code{X-calib.mzML} while the
SDRF names the acquired \code{X.raw}. Calls mzLib's \code{SdrfSearchScope.Restrict}, which joins them \strong{by
stem}, ignoring case, after removing the derivative suffixes MetaMorpheus writes (\code{-calib},
\code{-averaged}, in any order and number). Each kept row is carried cell for cell, with the searched
file's name in a new \code{comment[searched data file]} column directly after \code{comment[data file]};
nothing is renumbered.
}
\details{
\strong{Restrict first, then read a design.} \code{\link{sdrf_design}} and \code{\link{sdrf_isobaric_design}} match
\code{searched_files} by exact name, and key on \code{comment[searched data file]} when a document has it,
so a restricted document joins the search's own file names.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_restricted_sdrf}, which is also an \code{mzlibr_sdrf}, so \code{\link{sdrf_value}},
  \code{\link{sdrf_all}} and \code{\link{sdrf_records}} work on it. \code{path} is the SDRF restricted, as given;
  \code{columns} the restricted header, which may repeat names; \code{rows} one character vector of raw
  cells per returned row, in the input's row order - a kept row shorter than the header is padded
  with empty cells, so unlike \code{\link{sdrf_read}} the rows are never ragged. \code{row_count} counts the rows
  kept, in the \strong{whole} restricted document, whatever \code{limit} and \code{offset}; \code{returned_count} the
  rows returned, starting \code{offset} rows in; \code{truncated} whether rows were left behind;
  \code{input_row_count} the rows of the SDRF before the restriction.

  \code{searched_files} lists the searched files as sent, in order, and \code{acquired_name_count} counts
  the files you named an acquired file for. What the restriction could not place:
  \code{searched_without_row}, the searched files (file names) the SDRF has no row for - reported,
  never invented; \code{dropped_data_files}, the acquired files the SDRF describes that this search did
  not read (a row naming no file is dropped too, and not listed); \code{ambiguous}, acquisitions two or
  more searched files claimed, as \code{"stem: searched, searched"} - the first in name order kept the
  rows, and the others have none and are not in \code{searched_without_row}; and \code{shared_stem}, searched
  files whose rows name two or more \strong{different} acquired files (one run listed as \code{X.raw} and
  \code{X.mzML}), as \code{"searched: acquired, acquired"}, every such row kept. \code{written} is a list (\code{path},
  the file mzLib wrote, and \code{row_count}, the rows of the whole document), or \code{NULL} without \code{out}.
  \code{caveats} says how the join works and what it cannot tell you.

}
\section{Paths, on any platform}{


The searched files may be full paths; the join uses the file name. A path written with the other
platform's separator (\code{D:\\search\\x.mzML} on Linux) is reduced here too, but the design readers
split only the running platform's separator, so give \code{\link{sdrf_design}} and \code{\link{sdrf_isobaric_design}}
paths as this platform writes them (\code{file.path()} does).

}
